# Cursor AI Configuration for Binance Automation Framework
# ControlUp QA Automation Engineer Assessment

## Project Context
Language: C# (.NET 8.0)
Framework: NUnit 3.14 + FluentAssertions 8.8 + Moq 4.20
Architecture: Clean Architecture (Client → Services → Reporter)
Resilience: Polly (Exponential Backoff + Circuit Breaker)
DI Container: Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection

## Architectural Principles
- SOLID principles strictly enforced
- Interface-based design for all services
- Dependency injection throughout (no static dependencies)
- Fail-fast validation at API boundaries
- Graceful degradation for non-critical failures

## Code Generation Standards

### C# Conventions
- Async methods: Always include CancellationToken parameter
- Null safety: Use nullable reference types (enabled)
- Dispose pattern: Implement IDisposable for resource management
- Naming: Microsoft C# conventions (PascalCase public, _camelCase private)
- XML docs: Required for all public APIs

### Error Handling Philosophy
- Configuration errors: Fail-fast at startup (throw immediately)
- Transient failures: Retry with exponential backoff
- Partial failures: Log warning + graceful degradation
- Unrecoverable errors: Log error + propagate exception
- Never swallow exceptions silently

### Logging Requirements
- Use ILogger<T> for all logging
- Structured logging with semantic properties: `_logger.LogDebug("Message {Property}", value)`
- Levels: Debug (detailed flow), Info (milestones), Warning (recoverable), Error (failure)
- Include correlation IDs in distributed scenarios
- Log method entry/exit for complex flows

### Resilience Patterns
- HTTP clients must use Polly policies
- Retry policy: Exponential backoff (2^retry seconds), max 3 retries
- Handle: 5xx, 408 (timeout), 429 (rate limit)
- Circuit breaker: 5 consecutive failures, 30 second break
- Timeout: 30 seconds default, configurable via options

## Testing Standards

### Unit Tests (Category="Unit")
- Mock all external dependencies using Moq
- Use FluentAssertions for all assertions
- Test naming: `MethodName_Scenario_ExpectedBehavior`
- Structure: Arrange-Act-Assert (comments required)
- Coverage: Happy path + edge cases + error scenarios
- Verify mock interactions explicitly

### Integration Tests (Category="Integration")  
- Real HTTP calls to external APIs
- Handle geo-blocking gracefully (HTTP 451 → Assert.Ignore with message)
- Use TestConfiguration helper for DI setup
- Clean up resources in [OneTimeTearDown]
- Document expected CI behavior (may skip due to infrastructure)

### Test Quality Requirements
- Each test must have single responsibility
- Assertions must include descriptive failure messages
- Use TestCase for parameterized tests
- No Thread.Sleep - use proper async waiting
- Integration tests must be idempotent

## When Generating New Code

### Step 1: Interface First
```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Clear description of responsibility
/// </summary>
public interface IServiceName
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Method description
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="parameter">Parameter description</param>
    /// <param name="cancellationToken">Cancellation token</param>
    Task<ReturnType> MethodAsync(ParameterType parameter, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
}
```

### Step 2: Implementation with DI
```csharp
public class ServiceName : IServiceName
{
    private readonly IDependency _dependency;
    private readonly ILogger<ServiceName> _logger;

    public ServiceName(IDependency dependency, ILogger<ServiceName> logger)
    {
        _dependency = dependency ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(dependency));
        _logger = logger ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(logger));
    }
    
    // Implementation with error handling and logging
}
```

### Step 3: Registration
```csharp
// In ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs
services.AddScoped<IServiceName, ServiceName>();
```

### Step 4: Unit Tests
```csharp
[TestFixture]
[Category("Unit")]
public class ServiceNameTests
{
    private Mock<IDependency> _mockDependency;
    private Mock<ILogger<ServiceName>> _mockLogger;
    private ServiceName _service;

    [SetUp]
    public void Setup()
    {
        _mockDependency = new Mock<IDependency>();
        _mockLogger = new Mock<ILogger<ServiceName>>();
        _service = new ServiceName(_mockDependency.Object, _mockLogger.Object);
    }

    [Test]
    public async Task MethodAsync_ValidInput_ReturnsExpectedResult()
    {
        // Arrange
        // Act  
        // Assert
    }
}
```

## AI Assistant Behavior

When I ask you to generate code:
1. Define the interface first with XML documentation
2. Create implementation with constructor-based DI
3. Add comprehensive error handling (try-catch where appropriate)
4. Include structured logging at key decision points
5. Generate corresponding unit tests with Moq + FluentAssertions
6. Consider edge cases: null inputs, empty collections, network failures
7. Apply resilience patterns for external calls (Polly)

When I ask you to refactor code:
1. Preserve existing interfaces (breaking changes require explicit approval)
2. Maintain test coverage (update tests when behavior changes)
3. Improve error messages and logging clarity
4. Consider performance implications
5. Keep commits atomic and well-described

## Project-Specific Context

### External Dependencies
- RapidAPI (Binance43): May return HTTP 451 in CI environments (geo-blocking)
- Rate limiting: HTTP 429 handled via Polly retry policy
- Network failures: Transient errors expected, use circuit breaker

### Configuration Sources (Priority Order)
1. Environment variables (highest, used in CI/CD)
2. appsettings.Development.json (local, gitignored)
3. appsettings.json (defaults, committed)

### CI/CD Constraints
- GitHub Actions hosted runners may be geo-blocked
- Integration tests should skip gracefully with Assert.Ignore
- Secrets stored in GitHub Actions secrets
- Test results uploaded as artifacts (TRX files + JSON reports)
